Revised Y-042 change proposal — apex wave 2, lane R2
=====================================================

Status: PROPOSAL. Not applied. `tests/Oom.Tests/Scars/GetirmeScars.cs` is outside
this lane's OWNS list, and — more importantly — this patch must not land before
INT-3 produces the two evidence files it reads. Applying it today turns Y-042 red
on a missing file, which is an honest red but not a useful one.

Why this is not wave 1's patch
------------------------------
Wave 1 proposed pointing Y-042 at `bench/results/astra-dalga-1/recall-final.json`
and reading its thresholds. The apex review found three holes in that, and this
lane found the same three in the runner that would have produced the file:

1. It asserted `identity.executable.verified` — a boolean the runner writes about
   itself — and `binary_sha256` against `^[0-9a-f]{64}$`, which checks that a hash
   is hexadecimal. Neither compares the measured binary to an accepted one.
2. It read `thresholds.recall@3` / `thresholds.recall@5` out of the file it was
   grading. A file that lowers its own block passes.
3. It measured a corpus copy containing `knowledge/concepts/*.md` only, while
   `Retrieve.Corrections()` also reads `<vault>/<companion>/Duzeltmeler.md` and
   `Configuration.ReadRetrieve` reads five ranking keys out of `.oom/oom.json`.

And it did not separate the indexed path from the full scan at all.

`bench/verify_recall.py` (schema 2) closes all six. This patch is the consumer
side: what Y-042 must check in the evidence that runner emits.

Preconditions before applying
-----------------------------
- INT-3 has fixed the unified candidate and written its candidate manifest —
  a file naming the accepted `source_commit` and the published exe's SHA-256,
  authored in the lane that publishes, not in the lane that measures.
- Two runs of `bench/verify_recall.py run` against that same exe:

    python bench/verify_recall.py run \
      --exe <published oom.exe> \
      --candidate-manifest <manifest> \
      --vault "<owner's vault>" \
      --snapshot bench/.data/int3/snapshot \
      --gold  .brief/gold-sorular.jsonl \
      --expect-gold-sha256 d140ba3a624d574d... \
      --index-mode full-scan \
      --index-witness-note <note> --index-witness-query "<term> <shared term>" \
      --raw bench/results/astra-dalga-2/raw/final-full-scan.stdout.txt \
      --out bench/results/astra-dalga-2/recall-final-full-scan.json

  and the same again with `--index-mode indexed` (after `oom sweep`/`oom compile`
  has built the index), writing `recall-final-indexed.json`. The snapshot
  destination must be empty or `--snapshot-reuse`d; the runner will not delete it.
- Scar ledger rows for Y-250..Y-256 exist in `docs/scars.md` (see R2.md), so
  Y-125 is green again.

Cross-references
----------------
`tests/Oom.Tests/RecallCandidateBindingTests.cs` (this lane) already pins the
contract itself — the manifest binding, the constant thresholds, the input
inventory, the correction-id check, the before/after comparison, the two paths,
and the witness probe against the real ranker — against committed fixture
evidence. This patch is what makes Y-042 itself, the scar about a retrieval
change regressing recall, enforce that contract on the product's own numbers.

